    {
      "id": "how-it-works-detail",
      "type": "face",
      "label": "How it works · Reading page",
      "position": {
        "x": -120,
        "y": -700
      },
      "width": 760,
      "height": 470,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "how-it-works",
          "nodeId": "how-it-works-detail"
        },
        "view": {
          "intent": "node",
          "payload": "node.web.detail",
          "scroll": "page"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style=\"container-type:inline-size;box-sizing:border-box;width:100%;height:100%;overflow:auto;background:#faf9f6;color:#1b2e48;font-family:Georgia,serif;padding:6cqw\"><div style=\"font:900 2cqw system-ui;letter-spacing:.17em;color:#7957a4\">TWILITE / HOW IT WORKS</div><h1 style=\"font:600 8cqw/1.05 Georgia,serif;margin:4cqw 0\">Start with a node.<br>Build a world.</h1><p style=\"font:400 3.3cqw/1.48 Georgia,serif;color:#3c526d\">A Twilite document isn't a picture of connected things. It is the connected things: their content, relationships, reusable behaviors, and ways into other documents.</p><div style=\"border-top:1px solid #d5dce7;margin:5cqw 0;padding-top:4cqw;font:600 2.6cqw/1.55 system-ui\"><strong>1. Make</strong> an idea node. <strong>2. Connect</strong> it to a step. <strong>3. Reuse</strong> a class. <strong>4. Visit</strong> a separate graph through a portal.</div><p style=\"font:400 2.7cqw/1.5 system-ui;color:#52657d\">The example nodes on this canvas are real class instances. The relationships between them are real edges. The boutique is a real graph you can open, not a mockup.</p></div>"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "how-it-works-summary",
